This primary focus of this note is to analyze how geographies shape the colonies and eventually lead to their independence. Through the growing economic differences between the North and the South from geographies, the Civil War may seem inevitable to an extent.

Note that certain factors before the emergence of the colonies in North America results in large inertia that shapes certain aspects of the colonies. For example, enlightenment ideals motivates reason and democracy, leading to the justification of the legitimacy of independence to be based on "all men are created equal." This factor in the justification the legitimacy of the government of this civilization is far-reaching in its history. Moreover, beliefs regarding representation power and fear of power centralization, extending from the British history, impedes the establishment of a strong government in this nation, even centuries after its independence.

Mercantilism is among such prior beliefs that is difficult to shift once the society is adhering to its principles. Still, even if the society does not start with mercantilism beliefs, in the case where the international relations are not fully cooperative (eg. ideal communism) as such principles strengthens its nation.  Hence, the generalized mercantilism would hold for most likely all mother nations capable of attempting colonization. Rather than acquiring the most gold and silver, though, the general necessary but scarce resource would be the target to accumulate, as doing so is a positive feedback.

Also note that due to the difficulty in transportation between the old and the new world, it is a probable inference that there exists significant differences between the physical conditions of the colony and the mother nations (thereby the Mongols' far-reaching conquers are not colonialism).

The key result of colonialism from these assumptions is that the colonists will evolve into a different population, as defined by their shared beliefs, to the mother country. This makes sense as the first land-based plants today are far different (from biological categorization's point of view) to the water-based plant, and homo genus expanding from one geographical location to the next become different species, in The Three-Body Problem, it is described as "those first fish that climbed onto the land ceased to be fish."

